    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d278">

                <pb facs="0672" n="638" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v13/pg638"/>

                <head>278. Telegram From the Department of State to the
                        Embassy in Tunisia<anchor n="1" xml:id="d278-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1958-05-22T17:58:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 22,
                                1958—5:58 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>985. While Department doing everything possible with GOF, GOM and GOT to induce them avoid incidents present critical
                    juncture, representations in their direction unlikely affect decisions of FLN. Latter undoubtedly examining present situation
                    with view drawing <pb facs="0673" n="639" xml:id="pg639"/> every possible
                    advantage from it, and question therefore arises as to what if anything can be
                    done to restrain FLN from undertaking actions
                    which would involve Tunisia and Morocco in direct conflict with France.</p>
                <p>We note from Tunis 1583<anchor n="2" xml:id="d278-f2"/> that FLN units falling back from frontier into interior
                    of Tunisia. While this step will help avoid incidents on frontier, it will put
                        FLN in position to provoke French-Tunisian
                    clash inside country itself.</p>
                <p>We wonder whether in these circumstances you would deem it useful to get across
                    to FLN idea they would be well advised to
                    undertake no action which would further complicate existing Tunisian and
                    Moroccan problems. As Bourguiba has in past indicated
                    desire to transmit US views to FLN, we would
                    prefer use him in this instance rather than make direct comment to FLN which they might construe as official US
                    démarche.</p>
                <p>If you perceive objection or doubt usefulness such expression by us, Department
                    would be pleased to have your comment.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d278-f3"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                            >Dulles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d278-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d278-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 751S.00/5–2258. Secret; Limit
                    Distribution. Drafted by Porter, cleared
                    by Torbert, and approved and signed for
                        Dulles by Palmer. Repeated to Paris, Rabat, and
                    London.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d278-f2" n="2" xml:id="d278-f2-content">
                <p>Telegram 1583, May 19, reported the substance of a call by Ladgham and Mokaddem on Ambassador Jones. (<hi rendition="#italic">Ibid.,</hi> 651.72/5–1958)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d278-f3" n="3" xml:id="d278-f3-content">
                <p>Telegram 1630 from Algiers, May 24, questioned the advisability of attempting to
                    transmit to the FLN through
                        Bourguiba U.S. views on questions relating to FLN activities within Tunisia itself, as
                    distinguished from U.S. views on broader subjects. (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Ibid.,</hi> 751S.00/5–2458)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
